: "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the mild fever (99.2°F) and recent alcohol intoxication suggest alcohol-induced colonic inflammation/infection as a cause?", "result_summary": "Mild fever combined with alcohol history weakly suggests transient colonic irritation or infection, though this is less specific than the vascular malformation hypothesis.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1": "neutral", "B2": "neutral", "B3": "weak_for", "B4": "neutral", "B5": "neutral"}}, {"timestep": 1, "bundle_id": 1, "bundle_position": 3, "bundle_size": 5, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Could Mallory-Weiss syndrome (upper GI tear from vomiting) manifest as hematochezia without melena, creating ambiguity with B2?", "result_summary": "Mallory-Weiss syndrome typically presents with hematemesis, but rare delayed gastrointestinal transit could theoretically result in rectal blood; this weakens the case for vascular malformation while slightly supporting Mallory-Weiss.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1": "weak_for", "B2": "weak_against", "B3": "neutral", "B4": "neutral", "B5": "neutral"}}, {"timestep": 1, "bundle_id": 1, "bundle_position": 4, "bundle_size": 5, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "What evidence is MOST INCONSISTENT with Upper Gastrointestinal Bleed (Acute)? Identify the single strongest finding that argues against this diagnosis.", "result_summary": "The rectal presentation of bright red blood with no hematemesis is the strongest evidence against acute upper GI bleeding, as melena/hematochezia from upper sources requires significantly slower transit to allow darkening.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1": "strong_against", "B2": "neutral", "B3": "neutral", "B4": "neutral", "B5": "neutral"}}, {"timestep": 2, "bundle_id": 2, "bundle_position": 0, "bundle_size": 2, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does normal abdominal exam with no tenderness or signs of obstruction/mass refute gastrointestinal malignancy while supporting isolated vascular malformation as the most plausible source?", "result_summary": "Normal abdominal exam with no obstruction or mass strongly supports ruling out malignancy (B7) and indirectly favors isolated vascular malformation (B2) as the most plausible, localized cause of hematochezia.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1": "moderate_against", "B2": "strong_for", "B3": "neutral", "B7": "strong_against"}}, {"timestep": 2, "bundle_id": 2, "bundle_position": 1, "bundle_size": 2, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Could the presence of recent alcohol-induced vomiting with esophageal tear (Mallory-Weiss) explain rectal hemorrhage through retrograde bleeding patterns or diagnostic mislocalization?", "result_summary": "While Mallory-Weiss syndrome (B1) is an upper GI source, the theoretical possibility of hematochezia due to rapid transit creates weak support for B1 and introduces mild doubt about B2 as the exclusive explanation.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1": "weak_for", "B2": "weak_against", "B3": "neutral"}}], "differential_history": [{"B1": 0.18518518518518517, "B2": 0.41666666666666663, "B3": 0.3333333333333333, "B4": 0.046296296296296294, "B5": 0.018518518518518517}, {"B1": 0.18939374569579034, "B2": 0.4602268020407707, "B3": 0.3409087422524226, "B6": 1.022726226757268e-06, "B7": 0.009469687284789518, "B2.1": 0.2761360812244624, "B2.2": 0.1380680406122312, "B2.3": 0.04602268020407707}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "No instability markers present; hemodynamic parameters stable; no time-critical syndromes requiring immediate action.", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "B2 remains the leading hypothesis (posterior=0.46) but has not met the confirmation threshold (needs posterior ≥0.75). Dangerous alternative B1 (posterior=0.189, danger=0.5) remains unresolved. Available discriminators like colonoscopy and history of prior GI bleeding are unimplemented but could clarify the source."}, "turn_budget_used": 2, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.4, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.3409087422524226, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_vignette": "A 43-year-old man presents to the emergency department with bright red blood in his stool this morning. He noticed this yesterday as well, but it was much scanter. The patient is otherwise healthy. He was celebrating his birthday last night and admits having \"too much to drink\" and vomited profusely overnight. He currently feels well and is not nauseous. He has no other significant medical history and does not take any medications. His temperature is 99.2°F (37.3°C), blood pressure is 110/75 mmHg, pulse is 82/min, respirations are 16/min, and oxygen saturation is 100% on room air. Physical exam reveals a nontender abdomen with normal bowel sounds. Rectal exam reveals grossly bloody stool. Laboratory studies are ordered as shown below.\n\nHemoglobin: 12 g/dL\nHematocrit: 36%\nLeukocyte count: 5,500/mm^3 with normal differential\nPlatelet count: 179,000/mm^3\n", "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely cause of this patient's symptoms?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Erosion into a gastric vessel"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Inflammation and infection of colonic outpouching"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Malignancy"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Submucosal esophageal tear"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Vascular malformation"}], "static_evidence_items": [{"id": "E1", "kind": "direct", "content": "Bright red blood in stool this morning and yesterday", "source_ids": ["A"], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E2", "kind": "direct", "content": "Patient is 43-year-old man", "source_ids": ["1"], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E3", "kind": "direct", "content": "Otherwise healthy with no significant medical history", "source_ids": ["2"], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E4", "kind": "direct", "content": "Birthday celebration with excessive alcohol consumption and profuse vomiting overnight", "source_ids": ["3"], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E5", "kind": "direct", "content": "Vitals: temp 99.2°F (37.3°C), BP 110/75 mmHg, pulse 82/min, respirations 16/min, O2 sat 100% RA", "source_ids": ["4"], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E6", "kind": "direct", "content": "Physical exam: nontender abdomen with normal bowel sounds", "source_ids": ["5"], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E7", "kind": "direct", "content": "Rectal exam: grossly bloody stool", "source_ids": ["6"], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E8", "kind": "direct", "content": "Hemoglobin: 12 g/dL", "source_ids": ["7"], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E9", "kind": "direct", "content": "Hematocrit: 36%", "source_ids": ["7"], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E10", "kind": "direct", "content": "Leukocyte count: 5,500/mm³ with normal differential", "source_ids": ["7"], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E11", "kind": "direct", "content": "Platelet count: 179,000/mm³", "source_ids": ["7"], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'Rectal bleeding', 'Intestinal hemorrhage', 'Gastrointestinal bleeding'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "max_tree_depth": 3, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=60%, source=both]\n\nUpper Gastrointestinal Bleed (Acute) vs Colonic Angiodysplasia:\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, chest pain, difficulty breathing, drop in blood pressure, fainting\n  Favours second: abnormal bleeding, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cornea morphology, abnormal cutaneous elastic fiber morphology, abnormal facial shape\n\nUpper Gastrointestinal Bleed (Acute) vs Rectal Vascular Malformation:\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, black tarry stool, chest pain, difficulty breathing, drop in blood pressure\n  Favours second: abnormal bone ossification, abnormal occipital bone morphology, abnormal pelvis bone morphology, abnormality of ethmoid bone, abnormality of facial skeleton\n\nColonic Angiodysplasia vs Rectal Vascular Malformation:\n  Favours first:  abnormal bleeding, abnormal cardiac ventricular function, abnormal cornea morphology, abnormal cutaneous elastic fiber morphology, abnormal facial shape\n  Favours second: abnormal bone ossification, abnormal occipital bone morphology, abnormal pelvis bone morphology, abnormality of ethmoid bone, abnormality of facial skeleton\n\nRelated to Colonic Angiodysplasia: angiodysplasia of intestine, angiodysplasia of stomach, digestive system disease\n\nRelated to Rectal Vascular Malformation: blue rubber bleb nevus, bockenheimer syndrome, capillary malformation\n\n[RAG context for 'A 43-year-old man presents to the emergency department with bright red blood in his stool this morning' (score=0.68):]\n  Source: InternalMed_Harrison\n  Bright red blood passed with or on formed brown stool usually has a rectal, anal, or distal sigmoid source (Fig. 345-48). Patients with even trivial amounts of hematochezia should be investigated with\n[RAG context for 'A 43-year-old man presents to the emergency department with bright red blood in his stool this morning' (score=0.70):]\n  Source: Epidemiology and outcome of patients hospitalized with acute lower gastrointestinal hemorrhage: a population-based study. > Introduction\n  Hematochezia is the passage of frank blood per rectum, with or without stools. Although benign anorectal causes such as hemorrhoids and anal fissures qualify as hematochezia, clinicians often reserve \n[RAG context for 'He noticed this yesterday as well' (score=0.74):]\n  Source: Vascular malformations of the gastrointestinal tract. > Histopathology\n  Angiodysplasias are often challenging to diagnose in pathologic specimens. In resected specimens examined while fresh, one may visualize only a small focus of enhanced vascular markings and erythema, \n[RAG context for 'He noticed this yesterday as well' (score=0.75):]\n  Source: Causes of bleeding and outcomes in patients hospitalized with upper gastrointestinal bleeding. > Evaluation\n  Angiography is the gold standard diagnostic and therapeutic method directed toward the celiac trunk, superior mesenteric artery, and tributaries. Angiography identifies the causative artery and allows\n[Indirect reasoning chains (clinical inference):]\n  profuse vomiting after excessive alcohol consumption → Mallory-Weiss tear → Upper Gastrointestinal Bleed (Acute)\n    (frequency: frequent)\n    Suggestion: Consider acute Mallory-Weiss syndrome from alcohol- and vomiting-induced esophageal-mucosal tears as a mechanism explaining rectal bleeding despite upper GI origin, which could present as hematochezia in rapid transit\n  rectal bright red blood with minimal prior symptoms in a patient with alcohol use → acute alcohol-induced rectal mucosal damage → Upper Gastrointestinal Bleed (Acute)\n    (frequency: rare)\n    Suggestion: Investigate whether rectal blood could represent mucosal trauma from alcohol-induced rectal irritation and forceful vomiting, creating an atypical upper GI bleed presentation\n\nWhen indirect_reasoning_chains are present, generate candidates that investigate the INTERMEDIATE phenotype to confirm or rule out the indirect association."}
